 {
  "id": "art-0024-sauna-mental-health-research-2026",
  "slug": "sauna-heat-therapy-mental-health-depression-research-2026",
  "headline": "Scientists Are Putting Sauna Heat to the Test as a Possible Therapy for Depression",
  "socialHeadline": "From floating saunas in Oslo to clinical trials in the US, researchers are testing whether heat can ease depression \u2014 early results are promising but preliminary",
  "summary": "Researchers in Oslo and the United States are studying whether sauna and other heat therapies can help with depression and mental health. A small landmark trial found that raising body temperature reduced depressive symptoms within a week, and larger trials are now under way \u2014 though scientists stress the evidence is still early.",
  "body": [
   "The idea that heat can soothe the mind is ancient, but the science is only now catching up. From floating sauna villages in Oslo's harbor to clinical trials at American universities, researchers are investigating whether deliberate heat exposure could become a legitimate tool for treating depression and other mental health conditions.",
   "In Norway, cognitive neuroscientist Eva Hilland of Oslo Metropolitan University is recruiting 50 people for a randomized controlled trial, funded by a local municipality. Instead of a laboratory, participants will visit floating saunas in Oslo's inner harbor twice a week, combining the heat sessions with simple mental health habits. The study will measure stress, well-being, sleep and more.",
   "\"There are many knowledge gaps,\" Hilland told NPR, but the evidence she found while scanning the literature \"seemed promising. So we just saw the potential.\"",
   "## Why heat might matter for mood",
   "Heat appears to act on the brain through several pathways. Christopher Lowry, an integrative physiologist and neuroscientist at the University of Colorado Boulder, explains that when skin senses heat, the signals travel to a primitive part of the brain that controls cooling \u2014 a region that overlaps with a pathway involved in mood and pleasure, which is implicated in depression.",
   "Heat also triggers an anti-inflammatory response similar to the one produced by exercise, a well-established treatment for depression. And animal studies have found that heat activates a group of serotonin neurons that may provide stress resilience and antidepressant effects, Lowry said.",
   "The strongest human data come from a small landmark trial published a decade ago by a team at the University of Wisconsin-Madison. In that study, 30 people with depression were randomly assigned to either spend about two hours in a whole-body infrared device that raised their body temperature to about 38.5\u00b0C, or to a sham treatment. Within a week, a majority of the treated group had experienced a 50% reduction in symptoms, and the effect persisted through a six-week follow-up.",
   "## A 'thermoregulatory workout'",
   "Clinical psychologist Ashley Mason of the University of California, San Francisco, was drawn into the field by those findings. She noticed an intriguing pattern: people with depression tend to have difficulty with thermoregulation. \"What we know from a bunch of literature is that depressed people seem to run hot,\" she said. Her lab has tracked body temperature against depressive symptoms in wearable-device data from close to 20,000 people, finding that worse symptoms correlated with higher skin temperature and less daily temperature fluctuation.",
   "Heating the body may function like a \"thermoregulatory workout,\" Mason says \u2014 training the system to cool down more efficiently afterward. She has run two small pilot trials combining eight weeks of cognitive behavioral therapy with four sessions in an infrared hyperthermia machine; in both studies, most participants were in remission from depression by the end. Her team is now running the same protocol in a larger pool of patients at UCSF, Harvard and the Cleveland Clinic, funded by the National Institutes of Health and others.",
   "## Early days",
   "Scientists are careful to temper expectations. The evidence base largely rests on animal studies, cell models and a small number of human trials, most without strict control groups. Mason stresses that the pilot results are preliminary \u2014 the studies were not powered to show the treatments were effective \u2014 but they lay the groundwork for the larger, well-controlled trials that could.",
   "\"Heat does seem to be a promising therapy, but we're very much at the beginning of this research,\" she said. If future studies confirm the effect, heat therapy could earn a place alongside other non-pharmacological approaches for mental health, like exercise \u2014 \"a seat at the table\" for people whose depression has resisted other treatments, as Mason put it."
  ],
  "category": "Health",
  "tags": [
   "Mental Health",
   "Depression",
   "Sauna",
   "NPR",
   "Science"
  ],
