 {
  "id": "art-0026-super-el-nino-2026-record-2026",
  "slug": "super-el-nino-2026-record-climate-warming-2026",
  "headline": "Scientists Track a Potentially Record 'Super El Ni\u00f1o' as a Warming World Fuels the Extremes",
  "socialHeadline": "The 2026 El Ni\u00f1o is tracking hotter than the great events of 1997 and 2015 at the same point in the year \u2014 and scientists say it could push global temperatures to new records",
  "summary": "The El Ni\u00f1o building across the Pacific in 2026 is on track to be one of the strongest ever recorded, climate scientists say, with ocean temperatures already running hotter than the famous super El Ni\u00f1os of 1997 and 2015. Researchers warn it could drive record global heat in 2026 and 2027, amplified by the background of human-caused warming.",
  "body": [
   "The warming of the equatorial Pacific known as El Ni\u00f1o is developing into something extraordinary in 2026, and scientists are warning that it could shatter records. Analyses of ocean temperatures suggest the event is already tracking above the great El Ni\u00f1os of 1997 and 2015 at the same point in their development.",
   "\"No El Ni\u00f1o in the modern record has run this warm this early in its development,\" Berkeley Earth climate scientist Zeke Hausfather and chief scientist Robert Rohde wrote in a July 2026 temperature update. They estimate sea surface temperatures at the event's peak in December could run roughly 4\u00b0C above average in the key Ni\u00f1o regions \u2014 \"blowing the previous record out of the water.\"",
   "## What the agencies say",
   "The UK Met Office has called the developing event \"unprecedented,\" with its long-range GloSea forecast system showing sea surface temperatures in the Ni\u00f1o 3.4 region exceeding 3\u00b0C above normal in the coming months. Professor Adam Scaife, the Met Office's head of long-range forecasting, said he had \"never seen an El Ni\u00f1o signal this intense in our forecasts.\"",
   "The World Meteorological Organization has warned the phenomenon will last until at least February, with severe consequences for weather worldwide, according to Wired's September reporting.",
   "## Why it matters",
   "El Ni\u00f1os are a natural part of the climate cycle, but they now play out against a planet already warmed by greenhouse gases \u2014 and that makes them more destructive. The Met Office notes that the Indian summer monsoon is already running well below normal rainfall, with Central America, western Pacific nations and tropical South America also at risk of low rainfall. El Ni\u00f1o also tends to suppress Atlantic hurricanes by increasing wind shear; consistent with that pattern, there have been only three Atlantic storms so far this season, though the agency cautions that a single large hurricane can still cause devastation.",
   "For global temperatures, the outlook is stark. Rohde said during a July press briefing that it was \"now more likely than not\" that the extraordinary El Ni\u00f1o would bring record warmth in 2026 and \"much stronger warmth\" in 2027. Some analyses suggest the global average could climb to around 1.7\u00b0C above preindustrial levels by 2027 \u2014 above the 1.5\u00b0C threshold long treated as a guardrail.",
   "## The climate connection",
   "That is the focus of new reporting from Wired on how the 2026 super El Ni\u00f1o and climate change are related: El Ni\u00f1o is no longer just a temporary fluctuation but a force whose variability is intensified by a warming world, compounding cascading impacts.",
   "The warming will not last forever \u2014 Rohde expects 2028 to be cooler as the event subsides. But the background trend keeps climbing. \"Human emissions are adding a permanent El Ni\u00f1o worth of heat to the climate system each decade,\" Hausfather told The Washington Post. Even as El Ni\u00f1o's direct influence fades, the heat it borrows against the future does not fully return."
  ],
  "category": "Science",
  "tags": [
   "Climate",
   "El Ni\u00f1o",
   "Global Warming",
   "Weather",
   "Berkeley Earth"
  ],
  "author": "NewsVeria Newsroom",
  "publishedAt": "2026-10-11T10:21:25Z",
