 {
  "id": "art-0028-redwoods-heat-stress-study-2026",
  "slug": "california-redwoods-heat-stress-photosynthesis-study-2026",
  "headline": "California's Giant Redwoods Are Getting 'Heatstroke', a New Study Finds",
  "socialHeadline": "When temperatures spiked, the world's tallest trees slowed photosynthesis \u2014 the process that feeds them. UC Davis researchers say the findings raise alarms for the forests' future in a warming climate",
  "summary": "California's iconic coast redwoods \u2014 the tallest trees on Earth \u2014 slow the very process that feeds them when temperatures spike, according to a new University of California, Davis study. The findings, the first to measure heat's effect on redwood photosynthesis, raise concerns about the future of the forests as heatwaves grow more intense.",
  "body": [
   "The world's tallest trees are struggling to stay cool. When air temperatures topped 86\u00b0F in a Mendocino County redwood forest during the summers of 2022 and 2024, some redwood leaves ran nearly 20\u00b0F hotter than the air \u2014 a sign of stress that researchers had never fully measured before.",
   "The UC Davis study, led by PhD candidate Lily Klinek of the Plant Optics Lab in the Department of Plant Sciences and published in JGR Biogeosciences, tracked the trees over three summers. It found that under heat stress, coast redwoods slow photosynthesis \u2014 the chemical reaction that converts sunlight, water and carbon dioxide into the carbon they need to grow. \"It's as if the trees slow their 'breathing' and, as a result, their 'eating,'\" the researchers explain.",
   "\"The leaves aren't able to cool down,\" Klinek said \u2014 which suggests they closed their stomata, the tiny pores that take in carbon dioxide, to conserve water. Follow-up lab tests at temperatures up to 110\u00b0F confirmed that photosynthesis was reduced even when the leaves had plenty of water and kept their stomata open.",
   "## Why redwoods are vulnerable",
   "Redwoods evolved for cool coastal conditions, with average temperatures around 59\u00b0F. They also depend on coastal fog, which acts as a natural air conditioner. As heat spikes burn off that fog, the trees lose a key source of moisture \u2014 exactly when they need it most.",
   "The concern is greatest for redwood forests in already vulnerable central, southern and eastern parts of their range. \"We don't know what the upper temperature thresholds are for redwood photosynthesis and mortality,\" Klinek said. \"With this work, we see there's a significant impact of heat on photosynthesis and physiology. That's an important start for figuring out what parts of the redwood range are at risk.\"",
   "There is a sliver of good news: the trees the team studied did not lose their capacity to photosynthesize after the heat passed. \"Right now the temperatures are coming back to normal in the fall and winter and letting the trees recover,\" Klinek said. \"But in five or 10 years, if temperatures are even hotter and heat waves are even more frequent, we don't really know if there would be a recovery.\"",
   "Independent experts share the concern. UC Berkeley plant ecophysiologist Todd Dawson, who did not take part in the study, called it \"a double whammy\": \"They're running out of water and also getting this heat stress.\" He noted redwoods are adapted to a narrow band of 8 to 27\u00b0C, and that the coastal fog they rely on has declined by 30 to 40 percent.",
   "The study's authors include Troy Magney, Jessie Au and Milagros Rodriguez-Caton of UC Davis, Mukund Palat Rao of Columbia University, Lauren Fety of The Conservation Fund and George Koch of Northern Arizona University. It was funded by the National Science Foundation and California Forestry and Fire Protection, in cooperation with The Conservation Fund."
  ],
  "category": "Science",
  "tags": [
   "Climate",
   "Redwoods",
   "California",
   "Forests",
   "UC Davis"
  ],
