 {
  "id": "art-0029-iran-shopkeepers-war-economy-2026",
  "slug": "iran-shopkeepers-economy-war-inflation-tehran-2026",
  "headline": "\"Going Bankrupt\": Iran's Shopkeepers Struggle as War Wrecks the Economy",
  "socialHeadline": "At Tehran's Tajrish bazaar, customers who once bought nuts by the kilo now buy by the handful. With inflation near 90% and the currency collapsing, merchants say they are fighting for survival",
  "summary": "At the historic Tajrish bazaar in northern Tehran, the stalls are quieter than they used to be. Shoppers who once bought nuts by the kilogram now buy a half-kilo or less, as soaring prices driven by the US-Iran war eat into sales. Across the commercial district, many merchants say they are fighting for survival.",
  "body": [
   "Where fragrant herbs and spices once drew crowds through the narrow paths of the Tajrish bazaar, shops now sit mostly empty, according to an AFP dispatch carried by France 24. Nasir, a 61-year-old fruit and nut seller, said customers who \"used to buy two to five kilograms\" now buy one kilogram or half. \"Prices are truly high. We all know it, and it would be a lie to say otherwise,\" he said.",
   "The pressure comes from a war that began in February, the dispatch reports, involving US-Israeli attacks and an American blockade of Iran's ports \u2014 on top of years of sanctions that had already battered the economy.",
   "## Numbers in freefall",
   "Official figures cited in the dispatch paint a bleak picture. Inflation ran at nearly 90% year-on-year in September, and food prices have more than doubled since last year. Gross domestic product contracted 10.1% year-on-year between late March and late June, according to official data.",
   "The currency has collapsed with it: the rial traded at around 2.7 million to the dollar on the unofficial market in the past week, compared with about 1.7 million before the war. The minimum monthly wage, worth around $120 a year ago, has fallen to $65.",
   "## 'A desperate, last-ditch struggle'",
   "Ali Nowruzi, 36, who runs a sportswear shop in the bazaar, described the squeeze from both ends: \"It is a desperate, last-ditch struggle. Manufacturers are going bankrupt, laying off staff and closing shops, and the impact inevitably reaches us.\" He now orders only a few items for display, he said, so the shelves do not look as bare as his warehouse, which lies empty for lack of demand.",
   "\"We have absolutely no plan for the future. When people don't come to buy\u2026 it effectively spells bankruptcy for the business,\" he said, citing rising rent and utility bills. \"We just need some stability.\"",
   "The strain reaches far beyond the bazaar. Azadeh, a 45-year-old woman currently out of work, said a kitchen cloth that cost her 22 cents a few months ago now costs $1.10 \u2014 \"prices haven't just gone up, they've truly skyrocketed,\" she said. She has stopped buying some fruits and can no longer afford holidays. Car mechanic Mojtaba Rezaei, 57, told AFP his customers had \"dropped to a tenth of what it was\" because goods now cost two or three times as much \u2014 \"I actually feel embarrassed telling prices to customers.\"",
   "Iran's economy minister has rejected predictions of an imminent economic collapse, accusing the country's foes of trying to fuel public anxiety and drive up exchange rates, according to the dispatch.",
   "Independent reporting backs the broader trend: Euronews interviews with Iranians describe emptied wallets and collapsing purchasing power since the war began, while financial analysis has noted real-wage pressure across the region as the conflict disrupts energy and trade flows."
  ],
  "category": "World",
  "tags": [
   "Iran",
   "Economy",
   "Inflation",
   "Middle East",
   "AFP"
  ],
