 {
  "author": "NewsVeria Newsroom",
  "body": [
   "At least 109 migrants, including 15 children, have died after a boat carrying about 350 people capsized off the coast of Djibouti, the International Organization for Migration said on Sunday.",
   "The vessel went down during the night of October 8-9 near Guhere, north of the town of Obock, while sailing from Djibouti toward Yemen, according to the IOM. The agency said 109 bodies had been recovered so far: 52 men, 42 women and 15 children, nine of them girls and six boys. More than 116 people were still missing, and over 80 survivors \u2014 all Ethiopian nationals \u2014 had been pulled from the water.",
   "IOM teams are on the ground working alongside Djiboutian authorities, and survivors are being assisted at the agency's migrant response centre in Obock. \"This is a devastating loss of life,\" the IOM's chief of mission in Djibouti, Alexandre Coissac, said. Search and rescue operations were continuing on Sunday.",
   "The Djibouti-Yemen crossing is one of the world's busiest and most perilous migration corridors, used each year by tens of thousands of people \u2014 many from Ethiopia \u2014 attempting to reach Gulf states in search of work. Overloaded, unseaworthy boats make the journey routinely deadly; exact passenger counts often vary in the first hours after a disaster, with some outlets reporting between 305 and 350 people aboard this vessel."
  ],
