 {
  "author": "NewsVeria Newsroom",
  "body": [
   "The Pentagon said last week it plans to livestream the December 3 firing-squad execution of Nidal Hasan, the former Army psychiatrist convicted of the 2009 Fort Hood massacre \u2014 though the announcement has since been met with confusion and public doubt from within the administration itself.",
   "Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth told podcaster Jack Posobiec, in an interview recorded aboard the USS Abraham Lincoln on October 7: \"We'll make sure that people are able to watch it, that it's public.\" A Pentagon official subsequently confirmed the execution \"will be livestreamed.\" Acting Army Secretary Adam Telle set the date for December 3 at 1 p.m. local time, and President Trump approved Hegseth's recommendation.",
   "Hasan was convicted in 2013 of 13 counts of premeditated murder and 32 counts of attempted murder over the November 2009 shooting rampage at Fort Hood, Texas. If carried out, it would be the first US military execution in more than 60 years \u2014 and the first by firing squad since World War II.",
   "Alonzo Lunsford, a survivor who was shot seven times in the attack, told the BBC he supports the execution by firing squad and believes survivors and victims' families should be able to watch it uncensored.",
   "But the plan's future is uncertain. CNN reported on October 10 that Army and Pentagon officials were caught off guard by the announcement, and Vice President JD Vance publicly cast doubt on it, saying he did not know whether the livestream would actually happen."
  ],
