 {
  "id": "art-0036-commanders-giants-ot-tie-2026",
  "slug": "giants-commanders-27-27-overtime-tie-daniels",
  "headline": "Commanders Botch Final Play as Clock Runs Out, Settling for 27-27 Tie With Giants",
  "socialHeadline": "Jayden Daniels was sacked with 8 seconds left in OT and no timeouts \u2014 a 42-yard field goal never got snapped, and the Giants escaped Landover with a 27-27 tie",
  "summary": "The Washington Commanders reached the Giants' 24-yard line in overtime and were seconds from a winning field goal \u2014 but Jayden Daniels was brought down with no timeouts left, the clock expired, and the Week 5 game ended in a 27-27 tie at Northwest Stadium. Daniels took responsibility after the game.",
  "body": [
   "The Washington Commanders had the win in their hands. Driving into New York Giants territory in overtime on Sunday, October 11, they stood 24 yards from the end zone, 8 seconds on the clock, and a 42-yard field goal well within Drew Stevens' range. What followed was one of the most bewildering clock-management failures of the NFL season.",
   "Quarterback Jayden Daniels, on second-and-8 from the Giants' 24, dropped back, scrambled, and was tackled for no gain as the seconds bled away. With no timeouts remaining, the Commanders could not stop the clock or spike the ball. The officials ruled the overtime period over before Washington could run another play \u2014 and the Week 5 contest at Northwest Stadium in Landover, Maryland, finished as a 27-27 tie.",
   "\"We have to stop the clock,\" Daniels said afterward. \"We have to know the situation. It starts with everyone, top down. Ultimately, it's on me.\" Head coach Dan Quinn refused to put the sequence on his quarterback, saying the call and the clock management were his responsibility. Quinn said the tie \"feels like a loss.\"",
   "The ending overshadowed a dramatic game. Daniels, returning from a dislocated left elbow suffered in a Week 2 loss to Dallas, completed 17 of 25 passes for 199 yards and rushed for 103 yards and two touchdowns. Washington led 27-13 with just over seven minutes left in regulation before the Giants, behind Jameis Winston, rallied to force overtime.",
   "The tie moved Washington to 1-3-1 on the season, ahead of a Week 6 Monday night visit to the San Francisco 49ers. The Giants stood at 3-1-1. For a league in which ties remain one of the rarest results, this one will be remembered for what happened in the final eight seconds of overtime."
  ],
  "category": "Sports",
  "tags": [
   "NFL",
   "Washington Commanders",
   "New York Giants",
   "Jayden Daniels",
   "Overtime"
  ],
  "author": "NewsVeria Newsroom",
  "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T01:18:17Z",
