 {
  "id": "art-0039-big-ten-oregon-ucla-spot-2026",
  "slug": "big-ten-admits-error-oregon-ucla-2-point-conversion",
  "headline": "Big Ten Admits Officials Spotted Ball Wrong on Oregon's Do-or-Die 2-Point Try in 17-15 Loss to UCLA",
  "socialHeadline": "The conference said the ball should have been spotted at the half-yard line \u2014 not the 1.5-yard line \u2014 for Oregon's tying attempt with 2:16 left. The Ducks were stuffed, and UCLA held on to win at Autzen.",
  "summary": "The Big Ten admitted on Sunday that officials incorrectly spotted the ball on Oregon's tying 2-point conversion attempt with 2:16 left in a 17-15 loss to UCLA at Autzen Stadium. The ball was placed at the 1.5-yard line instead of the half-yard line after a UCLA facemask penalty \u2014 and the Ducks were stopped short.",
  "body": [
   "One yard. That was the difference the Big Ten admitted to on Sunday, October 11, a day after the Oregon Ducks fell 17-15 to the UCLA Bruins at Autzen Stadium.",
   "With 2:16 left in the fourth quarter, Oregon quarterback Brock Thomas ran up the middle on a first 2-point try and was stopped \u2014 but UCLA linebacker Sammy Omosigho grabbed his facemask, drawing a personal foul. The penalty should have moved the ball half the distance to the goal from the end of the run: the half-yard line. Instead, officials spotted it from the original line of scrimmage, placing it at the 1.5-yard line.",
   "In a statement released through ESPN's Pete Thamel, the conference confirmed the error. The ball should have been spotted, in the conference's words, half the distance to the goal from the end of the run of the first attempt \u2014 not from where the play started.",
   "Given the second chance from the 1.5-yard line, Oregon handed to running back Jordon Davison, who was stuffed by defensive tackle Tyson Ford short of the goal line. The Ducks' last-gasp lateral play on the final snap ended with a fumble recovered by UCLA's Dante Lovett.",
   "The Bruins improved to 5-0 and climbed to No. 12 in the Associated Press rankings, while Oregon fell to 3-2 \u2014 its second loss of the season. Whether the misplaced spot changed the outcome will be debated all week; the admission itself, rare for the conference, is already fueling the argument."
  ],
  "category": "Sports",
  "tags": [
   "College Football",
   "Oregon Ducks",
   "UCLA Bruins",
   "Big Ten",
   "Officiating"
  ],
  "author": "NewsVeria Newsroom",
  "publishedAt": "2026-10-12T01:18:17Z",
